    "main_topic": [
      {
        "h_title": "Surat Thani Areas Guide",
        "main_title": "Surat Thani Areas Guide",
        "author": null,
        "language": "en",
        "level": 1,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Which Neighborhood Fits Your Trip?",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Choosing where to stay in Surat Thani is mostly about one question: are you here for the city itself, or are you passing through on the way to somewhere else? Most travelers stay one night before a ferry, train, bus, or flight, so convenience usually matters more than hotel extras. The best base depends on how much you want to walk, eat well, and deal with onward transport.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "If you want the easiest stay, look at the central areas around Talat, the night market, and the riverfront. If you care more about a quiet night, suburban areas like Makham Tia or Khun Thale make more sense. And if your trip revolves around the train, Phunphin near the railway station is the practical option, even though it is not actually in the city center.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      },
      {
        "h_title": "Choosing Your Area",
        "main_title": "Surat Thani Areas Guide",
        "author": null,
        "language": "en",
        "level": 2,
        "primary_content": [
          {
            "text": "Stay in the city if you want better food, more services, and an easier evening on foot. Stay near the railway station in Phunphin only if you have a very early or very late train and want to cut transfer time.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Talat or the Bandon Pier / Tapi Riverfront area are usually the most practical. They give you easy access to travel agents, food, and morning transfers without being far from the city's main services.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Nightlife in Surat Thani is fairly modest compared with the islands or larger Thai cities. The night market and walking street area has the most evening energy for most travelers, but it is more about food and casual strolling than bars and clubs.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Khun Thale is usually the calmest choice, especially if you do not need to be in the center. Makham Tia can also work well for a quieter night while still being closer to city services.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Yes, especially in Talat or near the night market, where food and services are easier to reach on foot. In outer areas like Khun Thale or Makham Tia, having your own transport or using taxis regularly is much more useful.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          },
          {
            "text": "Most travelers only need one night. Two nights makes sense if you want a slower stopover, time to explore local food, or a buffer before a ferry, flight, or train connection during bad weather or a tight travel schedule.",
            "url": null,
            "urls": null
          }
        ],
        "secondary_content": null,
        "table_content": null
      }
    ],
